{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aad8e23e74682acf", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tLatent Softmax for Data-Efficient Phoneme-Based\n2\tMultilingual ASR Across Tonal and Non-Tonal\n3\tLanguages\n4\tSaierdaer Yusuyin1 † , Nanling Jiang2 † , Hao Huang1 , Zhijian Ou3,4 *\n5\t1\n6\t\n7\tarXiv:2608.01281v1 [eess.AS] 2 Aug 2026\n8\t\n9\t2\n10\t\n11\tSchool of Computer Science and Technology, Xinjiang University, China\n12\tDepartment of Electronic Engineering and Information Science, University of Science and Technology of China, China\n13\t3\n14\tSpeech Processing and Machine Intelligence (SPMI) Lab, Tsinghua University, China\n15\t4\n16\tTasiTech Co. Ltd., China\n17\tsar_dar@foxmail.com, huanghao@xju.edu.cn, ozj@tsinghua.edu.cn\n18\t\n19\tAbstract—Phoneme-based multilingual automatic speech\n20\trecognition (ASR) can share acoustic evidence across languages\n21\tmore directly than language-specific subword modeling. When\n22\ttonal and non-tonal languages are jointly trained, however, their\n23\tsupervision granularity does not match: tonal languages annotate tone-marked vowels, whereas non-tonal languages typically\n24\tprovide only base-vowel labels. A standard softmax either treats\n25\tthe two as unrelated classes, weakening cross-lingual sharing, or\n26\tcollapses tones, losing distinctions required by tonal languages.\n27\tWe propose Latent Softmax, a connectionist temporal classification (CTC)-compatible output layer that models tone-marked\n28\tvowels as subclasses and base vowels as major classes, while\n29\tconsonants and the CTC blank remain singleton labels. When\n30\tonly a base-vowel major-class label is observed, the tone-marked\n31\tvowel subclass is treated as latent and marginalized out. Multilingual experiments on AISHELL-1 Mandarin and LibriSpeech\n32\tEnglish show that Latent Softmax reduces speech-to-phoneme\n33\t(S2P) phoneme error rates over a standard softmax multilingual baseline by 8.4% on AISHELL-1, 17.5% on LibriSpeech\n34\ttest-clean, and 12.6% on test-other. The improved speech-tophoneme encoders also yield consistent word error rate gains\n35\tfor both large-language-model phoneme-to-grapheme conversion\n36\tand projector-based interfaces. After code-switching adaptation,\n37\tLatent Softmax further reduces projector-based mixed error rate\n38\tby 2.6% on ASRU2019 and 9.5% on CS-Dialogue datasets.\n39\tIndex Terms—multilingual ASR, phoneme-based ASR, tone\n40\tmodeling, Latent Softmax, CTC\n41\t\n42\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n43\tMultilingual and cross-lingual ASR are attractive because\n44\tdata from multiple languages can be pooled to improve\n45\ttraining efficiency, especially in low-resource settings. Recent\n46\tphoneme-based multilingual ASR has renewed this idea by\n47\tusing phonetic supervision as a language-agnostic interface.\n48\tCompared with language-specific subword units, phonemebased training encourages finer acoustic-phonetic sharing, and\n49\trecent work reports advantages in multilingual recognition,\n50\tcross-lingual transfer, and data efficiency [1]–[4]. Weakly\n51\tphonetic systems such as Whistle further show that phonetic\n52\tsupervision can be more data-efficient than subword supervision in multilingual and cross-lingual ASR [3].\n53\t* Corresponding author.\n54\t† Work done during internship at TasiTech.\n55\t\n56\tFor multilingual training involving both tonal and non-tonal\n57\tlanguages, a straightforward solution is to place all symbols\n58\tin one output inventory and treat a tone-marked vowel and\n59\tits non-tonal base-vowel counterpart as separate units. For\n60\texample, Mandarin /ā/ and English /a/ would occupy different\n61\tsoftmax outputs. This preserves Mandarin tone distinctions,\n62\tbut it limits direct sharing: English /a/ frames do not directly\n63\tprovide positive supervision to Mandarin /ā/, /á/, /ǎ/, and /à/,\n64\tand vice versa. The opposite strategy, collapsing all tonemarked vowel variants into the same base vowel, improves\n65\tsharing but removes distinctions that are essential for tonal\n66\tlanguages. The resulting research question is: is there a dataefficient mechanism for combined training of tonal and nontonal languages? Equivalently, how should tones be incorporated in multilingual phoneme-based models?\n67\tThe core challenge is that non-tonal vowel supervision\n68\tprovides only base-vowel major-class labels while the tonal\n69\tlanguage requires a finer tone-marked-vowel subclass space.\n70\tAt the same time, non-tonal speech is not pitch-free: it still\n71\texhibits pitch variation in production, but such variation is linguistically non-contrastive and therefore unmarked in phonetic\n72\ttranscription [5]–[7]. This suggests a latent-view interpretation\n73\tof non-tonal supervision: an English vowel token can be\n74\tregarded as an acoustic manifestation of one member of a\n75\ttone-marked vowel subclass family, even though that subclass\n76\tidentity is not annotated.\n77\tTo address this challenge, we propose Latent Softmax.\n78\tSoftmax is computed over subclasses. Base vowels are major\n79\tclasses; when only a base-vowel label is observed, the tonemarked vowel subclass is latent and marginalized. Tonemarked vowel labels remain explicit subclasses for tonal\n80\tlanguages. For non-singleton vowel groups, major classes have\n81\tno independent output neurons and are modeled by sums of\n82\tsubclass probabilities. Figure 1 illustrates the idea.\n83\tLatent Softmax enables more efficient sharing than standard\n84\tsoftmax in joint tonal/non-tonal training. For example, Mandarin /ā/ and English /a/ data can reinforce each other through\n85\ta shared base-vowel hierarchy: English /a/ spreads posterior\n86\tmass across compatible tone-marked vowel subclasses, while\n87\t\n88\t\fMandarin /ā/ sharpens one subclass. Thus, non-tonal vowels\n89\timplicitly augment the tone-marked subclass space. Bilingual\n90\texperiments on AISHELL-1 Mandarin [8] and LibriSpeech\n91\tEnglish [9] show improvements in both speech-to-phoneme\n92\t(S2P) and downstream word recognition with large-languagemodel phoneme-to-grapheme (LLM-P2G) conversion [10] and\n93\tprojector-based interfaces [11]. After code-switching adaptation, Latent Softmax further reduces mixed error rates on\n94\tASRU2019 and CS-Dialogue datasets.\n95\tII. R ELATED W ORK\n96\tA. Tone modeling in ASR\n97\tTone modeling is well studied for tonal-language ASR.\n98\tIn Chinese ASR, tone-marked vowels are commonly used\n99\tin acoustic modeling, often with fundamental-frequency (F0)\n100\tfeatures appended to frame-level spectral features [12], [13].\n101\tRecent CTC work revisits joint versus separate phone-tone\n102\tmodeling [14]; synchronous models using consonants and\n103\ttone-marked vowels achieve lower error rate on the joint\n104\tphone+tone tier [14]. These studies address tonal-language\n105\trepresentations, while we target mixed tonal/non-tonal training\n106\twhere non-tonal data have no tone labels.\n107\tTone-related representations have also been studied for nontonal languages [5]–[7], suggesting that pitch and tone-related\n108\tcues can improve non-tonal ASR. Latent Softmax instead\n109\tpromotes tonal/non-tonal sharing by marginalizing over tonemarked vowel subclasses when only base-vowel labels are\n110\tavailable, without adding tone features or predicting tone for\n111\tevery language.\n112\tB. Multilingual training involving both tonal and non-tonal\n113\tlanguages\n114\tPhoneme-based multilingual ASR commonly uses a shared\n115\tinventory, often based on the International Phonetic Alphabet\n116\t(IPA), to encourage cross-lingual sharing [1]–[4]. Weakly\n117\tphonetic systems such as Whistle show that IPA-based supervision can be more data-efficient than subword supervision,\n118\tbut mainly study non-tonal languages and identify tone incorporation as open [3].\n119\tSome related work involves tonal and non-tonal languages\n120\tbut addresses the problem differently. JoinAP links phone\n121\tembeddings via phonological features and evaluates CommonVoice European multilingual training with zero-shot and\n122\tfew-shot transfer to AISHELL-1 Mandarin [15]. Universal\n123\tphone recognition with a multilingual allophone system models language-dependent phonemes with language-independent\n124\tphones [16]. These approaches are related in spirit, but do not\n125\tdirectly address the supervision mismatch where tonal languages provide tone-marked vowels and non-tonal languages\n126\tprovide only base vowels.\n127\tMore generally, simply concatenating tone-marked and nontonal vowel units preserves discriminability but limits sharing\n128\tbetween base vowels and tonal variants. Latent Softmax fills\n129\tthis gap by using each base vowel as a major class over tonemarked subclasses, letting non-tonal base-vowel labels train\n130\tthe subclass space without tone annotations.\n131\t\n132\tC. Relation to hierarchical classification and partial supervision\n133\tLatent Softmax is also related to mixed-granularity or partial\n134\tsupervision outside speech. In hierarchical multi-granularity\n135\tclassification, a sample may have coarse or fine labels, and\n136\trecent losses maximize the observed label’s marginal probability by aggregating compatible descendants [17]. Coarselabel representation learning [18] and partial-label learning\n137\t[19] likewise treat supervision as a set of candidate fine labels.\n138\tOur formulation is closest to this marginalization view.\n139\tHowever, these formulations are not designed for CTC\n140\tsequence modeling or multilingual phoneme spaces mixing\n141\ttonal and non-tonal supervision. We introduce latent subclass\n142\tmarginalization into tonal/non-tonal multilingual S2P modeling and show that it improves data sharing in multilingual\n143\tASR.\n144\tIII. M ETHOD\n145\tA. Standard softmax for CTC-based S2P\n146\tLet L denote the label inventory used by a standard speechto-phoneme (S2P) model, including phoneme labels and the\n147\tCTC blank. Given acoustic input x, a standard softmax layer\n148\tproduces logits ut (l) for each l ∈ L and frame posterior\n149\texp(ut (l))\n150\t.\n151\t′\n152\tl′ ∈L exp(ut (l ))\n153\t\n154\tpt (l) = p(πt = l | x) = P\n155\t\n156\t(1)\n157\t\n158\tFor target label sequence y, the CTC likelihood is\n159\tP (y | x) =\n160\t\n161\tT\n162\tX Y\n163\t\n164\tpt (πt ),\n165\t\n166\t(2)\n167\t\n168\tπ∈A(y) t=1\n169\t\n170\twhere A(y) is the set of frame-level alignments that collapse\n171\tto y.\n172\tConsider phoneme-based multilingual training involving\n173\tboth tonal and non-tonal languages. Under standard softmax,\n174\ta Mandarin tone-marked vowel label such as /ā/ and an\n175\tEnglish base-vowel label such as /a/ are either treated as\n176\tseparate classes or manually collapsed. The former reduces\n177\tdata sharing; the latter discards tone distinctions. Latent Softmax changes only the output-probability computation, while\n178\tkeeping the CTC objective in Eq. (2) unchanged in form.\n179\tB. Latent Softmax formulation\n180\tLet Z be the subclass inventory. The softmax computation is\n181\tover subclasses. These subclasses include tone-marked vowels\n182\tfor tonal languages, together with singleton labels that do not\n183\tneed further subdivision, including consonants and the CTC\n184\tblank. For vowels, the corresponding base vowel defines a major class; for example, /ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/ belong to the major class\n185\t/a/. Consonants and the CTC blank remain singleton labels\n186\tand do not participate in this major/subclass decomposition.\n187\tEach training utterance comes with a target sequence y. We\n188\tuse the term supervised label for an element of this sequence,\n189\tnamely the label actually provided by the training corpus after\n190\tphoneme conversion. For a Mandarin utterance, a supervised\n191\tlabel may be a tone-marked vowel such as /ā/. For an English\n192\t\n193\t\fFig. 1. Overview of standard softmax and Latent Softmax for combined training of tonal and non-tonal languages. Standard softmax treats non-tonal base\n194\tvowels and tone-marked vowels as independent output classes. Latent Softmax keeps tone-marked vowels as subclasses. A tone-labeled sample supervises\n195\ta singleton tone-marked vowel subclass, while a non-tonal base-vowel sample supervises the base-vowel major class obtained by marginalizing compatible\n196\ttone-marked vowel subclasses; in this case the subclass identity is latent.\n197\t\n198\tutterance, a supervised label may instead be a base vowel\n199\tsuch as /a/. For each supervised label l, define the compatiblesubclass set\n200\tB(l) = {z ∈ Z : z is compatible with l}.\n201\t\n202\t(3)\n203\t\n204\tIf l is a non-tonal base vowel, then B(l) contains all tonemarked vowel subclasses of that base vowel; otherwise,\n205\tB(l) = {l}. The second case covers tone-marked vowels as\n206\twell as singleton labels such as consonants and blank.\n207\tThe network outputs subclass logits vt (z) and the subclass\n208\tposterior is\n209\texp(vt (z))\n210\t.\n211\t′\n212\tz ′ ∈Z exp(vt (z ))\n213\t\n214\tpt (z) = p(zt = z | x) = P\n215\t\n216\tThe frame-level probability used by CTC is then\n217\tX\n218\tqt (l) =\n219\tpt (z).\n220\t\n221\t(4)\n222\t\n223\t(5)\n224\t\n225\tz∈B(l)\n226\t\n227\tThus, an English base-vowel label /a/ uses B(/a/) =\n228\t{/ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/}, while a Mandarin tone-marked vowel label /ā/\n229\tuses B(/ā/) = {/ā/}, assuming four Mandarin tone-marked\n230\tvowel subclasses. In the phonetic representation used here,\n231\ttones are attached to the central vowel in a syllable. This is\n232\ta practical transcription choice: tones are suprasegmental and\n233\tcan extend beyond the vowel segment boundary, but they are\n234\tconventionally marked on the syllabic nucleus because their\n235\tmost salient effect is typically on the central vowel.\n236\tThe corresponding CTC likelihood becomes\n237\tP (y | x) =\n238\t\n239\tX\n240\t\n241\tT\n242\tY\n243\t\n244\tC. Gradient interpretation for Latent Softmax\n245\tFor analysis, define the aggregated logit of supervised label\n246\tl as\n247\tX\n248\tut (l) = log\n249\texp(vt (z ′ )).\n250\t(7)\n251\tz ′ ∈B(l)\n252\t\n253\tThen the frame-level probability used by CTC can be rewritten as\n254\texp(ut (l))\n255\t.\n256\t(8)\n257\tqt (l) = P\n258\t′\n259\t′\n260\tz ∈Z exp(vt (z ))\n261\tHere ut (l) is only a convenient aggregated logit used for\n262\tinterpretation; Latent Softmax does not introduce separate\n263\toutput neurons for major classes.\n264\tThe neural network outputs logits over subclasses. Consider\n265\tthe gradient of the log likelihood with respect to the logit\n266\tfor subclass z. The gradients with respect to the network\n267\tparameters can then be obtained from these logit gradients\n268\tby standard back-propagation. Let γt (l) = P (πt = l | x, y)\n269\tbe the CTC posterior of label l at frame t, obtained by the\n270\tforward-backward algorithm; and note that the same subclass\n271\tz may occur several times in a single utterance, i.e., there can\n272\tbe multiple supervised-label occurrences l in an utterance such\n273\tthat z ∈ B(l). Then\n274\t∂ log P (y | x) X\n275\t∂ log qt (l)\n276\t=\n277\tγt (l)\n278\t∂vt (z)\n279\t∂vt (z)\n280\tl\n281\tX\n282\t∂ut (l)\n283\t=\n284\tγt (l)\n285\t− pt (z)\n286\t∂vt (z)\n287\tl:z∈B(l)\n288\t\n289\t=\n290\t\n291\tX\n292\tl:z∈B(l)\n293\t\n294\tqt (πt ).\n295\t\n296\t(6)\n297\t\n298\tπ∈A(y) t=1\n299\t\n300\tThus, Latent Softmax can be implemented as a deterministic\n301\taggregation layer between the subclass softmax and the CTC\n302\tcriterion. Once the aggregated probabilities qt (l) are formed, a\n303\tstandard CTC forward-backward implementation can evaluate\n304\tthe loss, and automatic differentiation in toolkits such as PyTorch can backpropagate gradients to the network parameters.\n305\t\n306\tγt (l)\n307\t\n308\tpt (z)\n309\t− pt (z).\n310\tqt (l)\n311\t\n312\t(9)\n313\t\n314\tThe same result can also be obtained from a latent-subclass\n315\tview. Let zt be the latent subclass at frame t, and let z =\n316\tz1 , . . . , zT . By Fisher’s identity [20],\n317\t\u0015\n318\t\u0014\n319\t∂\n320\t∂\n321\tlog P (y | x) = EP (z|x,y)\n322\tlog pt (zt )\n323\t∂vt (z)\n324\t∂vt (z)\n325\t= EP (z|x,y) [δ(zt = z) − pt (z)]\n326\t= P (zt = z | x, y) − pt (z),\n327\t\n328\t(10)\n329\t\n330\t\fFig. 2. Phoneme-count distribution in LibriSpeech English and AISHELL-1 Mandarin. The horizontal axis lists English and Mandarin phoneme labels, ordered\n331\tby descending major-class count, and the vertical axis reports their corpus-level counts in the two datasets. When a phoneme appears in both English and\n332\tChinese, two bars are shown side-by-side. Base vowels and their tone-marked vowel subclasses, such as /a/, /ā/, /á/, /ǎ/, and /à/, are placed contiguously to\n333\tshow how non-tonal and tonal evidence is distributed across the shared inventory.\n334\t\n335\twhere the posterior assigned to subclass z is\n336\tP (zt = z | x, y)\n337\t(11)\n338\tX\n339\t=\n340\tP (πt = l | x, y)P (zt = z | x, y, πt = l)\n341\tl:z∈B(l)\n342\t\n343\t=\n344\t\n345\tX\n346\tl:z∈B(l)\n347\t\n348\tγt (l)\n349\t\n350\tpt (z)\n351\t.\n352\tqt (l)\n353\t\n354\t(12)\n355\t\n356\tThus Eq. (9) and Eq. (10) coincide.\n357\tThe two derivations emphasize complementary aspects of\n358\tthe method. The chain-rule view shows how the positive CTC\n359\tposterior mass of each supervised label is redistributed across\n360\tall compatible subclasses. The Fisher-identity view shows\n361\tthe probabilistic meaning: when only a base-vowel label is\n362\tobserved, the tone-marked vowel subclass is latent and its\n363\tposterior is inferred rather than fixed. Note that for labels\n364\tother than non-tonal base vowels, B(l) is a singleton and the\n365\tgradient reduces to the usual CTC-softmax form.\n366\tThis also gives a direct data-sharing interpretation. For\n367\ta non-tonal base-vowel label, the positive posterior mass is\n368\tdistributed across all compatible tone-marked vowel subclasses\n369\taccording to the current model belief. Therefore, non-tonal\n370\tvowel frames update the tone-marked vowel subclass space\n371\twithout requiring tone annotations. In a standard softmax,\n372\tMandarin /ā/ and English /a/ are separate output classes. In\n373\tLatent Softmax, English /a/ contributes posterior mass to the\n374\tcompatible subclass family {/ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/}, while Mandarin /ā/\n375\tsharpens one specific subclass inside the same major class.\n376\tThe two sources of data therefore mutually augment each other\n377\tthrough the shared base-vowel hierarchy.\n378\tD. Evaluation metrics\n379\tWe report tonal phoneme error rate (T-PER), non-tonal\n380\tphoneme error rate (NT-PER), word error rate (WER), and\n381\tmixed error rate (MER). Both tonal PER and non-tonal PER\n382\tare edit distances between decoded and reference phoneme\n383\tsequences, normalized by the reference phoneme length. Tonal\n384\tPER is computed on tone-marked phoneme sequences and is\n385\tused for the Mandarin AISHELL-1 test set. Non-tonal PER\n386\tis computed after removing tone marks from the decoded\n387\tand reference phoneme sequences; it is used for the English\n388\t\n389\tLibriSpeech test sets and for the Mandarin–English codeswitching test sets. This distinction is needed because the\n390\tLatent Softmax S2P model can decode tone-marked vowel\n391\tsubclasses even for English, while the English reference\n392\tphoneme sequence has no tone labels. WER is computed as the\n393\tnormalized edit distance between decoded and reference word\n394\tsequences. For Mandarin–English code-switching evaluation,\n395\twe use MER, where Mandarin characters and English words\n396\tare treated as mixed tokens for edit-distance scoring.\n397\tIV. E XPERIMENTS\n398\tA. Datasets and setup\n399\tWe conduct multilingual experiments by pooling data from\n400\tone tonal language (Mandarin) and one non-tonal language\n401\t(English). AISHELL-1 is used as the Mandarin data, containing approximately 178 hours of read speech from 400 speakers\n402\t[8]. LibriSpeech is used as the English data; it contains\n403\tapproximately 1000 hours of 16 kHz read speech, and we\n404\treport results on the official test-clean and test-other subsets\n405\t[9]. Text transcripts are converted to phoneme sequences using\n406\tthe same lexicon or grapheme-to-phoneme pipeline for all\n407\tcompared S2P systems.\n408\tWe further evaluate code-switching adaptation on two\n409\tMandarin–English code-switching datasets. The ASRU2019\n410\tMandarin-English code-switching challenge data contain 500\n411\thours of Mandarin-only speech, a 200-hour Train_CS set, a\n412\t40-hour Dev_CS set, and a 20.6-hour Test_CS set [21]. In\n413\tour code-switching fine-tuning experiments, the additional 500\n414\thours of Mandarin-only speech are not used. CS-Dialogue is\n415\ta spontaneous Mandarin-English dialogue corpus with 104.02\n416\thours of speech from 200 speakers and 38,917 utterances\n417\t[22]. It provides speaker-independent training, development,\n418\tand test splits of 68.97, 18.30, and 16.74 hours, respectively.\n419\tFigure 2 visualizes the empirical label distribution behind\n420\tthe mixed English–Mandarin training setup. The figure makes\n421\texplicit the supervision mismatch addressed by Latent Softmax: one base vowel can appear as a non-tonal English\n422\tlabel and as multiple Mandarin tone-marked vowel subclasses.\n423\tUnder a standard softmax these counts are consumed by\n424\tseparate output classes, whereas Latent Softmax lets non-tonal\n425\tbase-vowel observations train the corresponding base-vowel\n426\t\n427\t\fTABLE I\n428\tT ONAL PER S (%), NON - TONAL PER S (%), AND WER S (%) ON AISHELL-1 AND L IBRI S PEECH . AISHELL-1 USES TONAL PER, WHILE L IBRI S PEECH\n429\tUSES NON - TONAL PER. PER IS MEASURED AT THE S2P OUTPUT.\n430\tAISHELL-1\n431\tSystem\n432\t\n433\tLibriSpeech\n434\ttest-clean\n435\ttest-other\n436\tNon-tonal PER\n437\tWER\n438\tNon-tonal PER\n439\tWER\n440\t\n441\tTonal PER\n442\t\n443\tWER\n444\t\n445\tStandard Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\n446\tStandard Softmax S2P + Projector\n447\t\n448\t2.03\n449\t\n450\t5.63\n451\t5.61\n452\t\n453\t1.60\n454\t\n455\t4.29\n456\t3.92\n457\t\n458\t5.00\n459\t\n460\t10.70\n461\t9.61\n462\t\n463\tLatent Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\n464\tLatent Softmax S2P + Projector\n465\t\n466\t1.86\n467\t\n468\t5.26\n469\t5.52\n470\t\n471\t1.32\n472\t\n473\t3.91\n474\t3.63\n475\t\n476\t4.37\n477\t\n478\t9.89\n479\t9.14\n480\t\n481\tTABLE II\n482\tN ON - TONAL (NT) PER S (%) AND MER S (%) ON M ANDARIN –E NGLISH\n483\tCODE - SWITCHING DATASETS AFTER CODE - SWITCHING ADAPTATION .\n484\tSystem\n485\t\n486\tASRU2019\n487\tNTMER\n488\tPER\n489\t\n490\tCS-Dialogue\n491\tNTMER\n492\tPER\n493\t\n494\tStandard Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\n495\tStandard Softmax S2P + Projector\n496\t\n497\t3.23\n498\t\n499\t13.69\n500\t14.08\n501\t\n502\t9.66\n503\t\n504\t15.53\n505\t\n506\tLatent Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\n507\tLatent Softmax S2P + Projector\n508\t\n509\t3.83\n510\t\n511\t13.83\n512\t13.71\n513\t\n514\t9.02\n515\t\n516\t14.06\n517\t\n518\tmajor class and, through marginalization, the associated tonemarked vowel subclass family.\n519\tThe S2P encoder follows a Conformer-based configuration\n520\tcomparable to the Whistle-small setting [3]. We use Qwen31.7B as the LLM. LoRA uses rank r = 1024 and LoRA alpha\n521\tα = 2048. The projector is a two-layer 4096-dimensional\n522\tlinear projection with a ReLU activation between the two\n523\tlinear layers. We compare a standard softmax multilingual\n524\tS2P baseline and the proposed Latent Softmax S2P model.\n525\tThe standard softmax baseline treats the multilingual output\n526\tinventory as flat labels. Latent Softmax uses the same encoder\n527\tarchitecture but replaces the flat output probability with the\n528\tmarginalization layer in Eq. (5). Downstream word recognition\n529\tis evaluated with two speech-language interfaces: an LLMP2G system that converts S2P phoneme sequences to text [10],\n530\tand a projector-based system that maps S2P representations to\n531\tlarge-language-model token embeddings [11].\n532\tIn the main experiments, the LLM-P2G interface trains the\n533\tS2P model and the LLM-P2G model in two separate stages.\n534\tThe projector interface adopts a three-stage pipeline: (1) S2P\n535\tis trained first; (2) after removing the S2P linear classifier,\n536\tthe projector is trained while the S2P encoder and LLM are\n537\tfrozen; (3) finally, the LLM LoRA parameters are trained with\n538\tboth the S2P encoder and projector fixed.\n539\tCode-switching adaptation reuses the same stage-wise protocol. All modules are initialized from the main experiment,\n540\tand the projector is kept frozen throughout. For the projector\n541\tinterface, this means fine-tuning S2P first and then the LLM\n542\tLoRA parameters. For the LLM-P2G interface, S2P and LLMP2G are fine-tuned separately in the same order.\n543\tB. Main results\n544\tTable I summarizes the main results. Latent Softmax reduces S2P PER from 2.03% to 1.86% on AISHELL-1, from\n545\t1.60% to 1.32% on LibriSpeech test-clean, and from 5.00%\n546\tto 4.37% on test-other. These correspond to relative PER\n547\t\n548\treductions of 8.4%, 17.5%, and 12.6%, respectively, over\n549\tthe standard softmax multilingual baseline. The AISHELL-1\n550\tnumber is tonal PER, and the LibriSpeech numbers are nontonal PERs.\n551\tThe downstream WERs show the same general trend. With\n552\tLLM-P2G, Latent Softmax improves WER from 5.63% to\n553\t5.26% on AISHELL-1, from 4.29% to 3.91% on test-clean,\n554\tand from 10.70% to 9.89% on test-other. With the projector\n555\tinterface, it improves WER from 5.61% to 5.52%, from\n556\t3.92% to 3.63%, and from 9.61% to 9.14%. The largest WER\n557\tgains occur in the LLM-P2G setting, where the phoneme\n558\tsequence emitted by S2P directly determines the P2G input.\n559\tThe projector interface is already strong on LibriSpeech, so\n560\tthe absolute gains are smaller, but Latent Softmax remains\n561\tconsistently better than the corresponding standard softmax\n562\tcounterpart.\n563\tIt is important not to overstate a single downstream interface\n564\tas universally best. Within the Latent Softmax systems, LLMP2G gives the lowest WER on AISHELL-1 (5.26%), whereas\n565\tthe projector interface gives lower WERs on LibriSpeech\n566\t(3.63% on test-clean and 9.14% on test-other). The main\n567\tconclusion from Table I is therefore not that one interface\n568\tdominates, but that Latent Softmax improves the shared S2P\n569\tencoder and that the improvement transfers to both downstream ASR interfaces.\n570\tC. Fine-tuning on Code-switching Datasets\n571\tThe AISHELL-1 and LibriSpeech evaluations above pool\n572\tMandarin and English data, but each utterance is singlelanguage rather than code-switched. We therefore further\n573\tconsider Mandarin–English code-switching, a more difficult\n574\tsetting where languages may mix within an utterance. Table II reports adaptation results obtained by fine-tuning the\n575\tcorresponding systems in Table I. We evaluate both LLM-P2G\n576\tand projector interfaces on ASRU2019. For CS-Dialogue, the\n577\tLLM-P2G training was unstable and is therefore omitted, as\n578\tdiscussed below.\n579\tOn ASRU2019, the two downstream interfaces behave\n580\tdifferently. With LLM-P2G, standard softmax S2P gives a\n581\tslightly lower MER than Latent Softmax (13.69 vs. 13.83),\n582\tconsistent with its lower non-tonal PER (3.23 vs. 3.83).\n583\tWith the projector interface, however, Latent Softmax reduces\n584\tMER by 2.6% relative to standard softmax (14.08 vs. 13.71),\n585\tsuggesting that the projector approach still benefits from the\n586\ttone-aware representation learned by Latent Softmax.\n587\t\n588\t\fTABLE III\n589\tE XAMPLE SUBCLASS AND MAJOR - CLASS PHONEME DECODING ON L IBRI S PEECH . S UBCLASS SEQUENCES ARE DECODED FROM SUBCLASS POSTERIORS ;\n590\tMAJOR - CLASS SEQUENCES ARE DECODED AFTER MARGINALIZING SUBCLASS POSTERIOR PROBABILITIES .\n591\ttext\n592\t\n593\tsubclass phoneme sequence\n594\t\n595\tmajor class phoneme sequence\n596\t\n597\twhere is that\n598\tyes i know very well\n599\twhat made the difference\n600\t\n601\tw è õ í z ð æ th\n602\tj è s á í n ó Ú v @` õ í w è l\n603\tw @` th m è ı̄ d ð @ˇ d ì f @ˇ õ @ˇ n s\n604\t\n605\tw e õ i z ð æ th\n606\tjesainoUv@õiwel\n607\tw @ th m e i d ð @ d i f @ õ @ n s\n608\t\n609\tTABLE IV\n610\tWER S (%) OF THE LLM-P2G MODEL WHEN USING SUBCLASS AND\n611\tMAJOR - CLASS S2P PHONEME SEQUENCES AS INPUT, RESPECTIVELY.\n612\tS2P output\n613\t\n614\tAISHELL\n615\t\n616\tLS clean\n617\t\n618\tLS other\n619\t\n620\tMajor class\n621\tSubclass\n622\t\n623\t5.31\n624\t5.26\n625\t\n626\t4.10\n627\t3.91\n628\t\n629\t10.19\n630\t9.89\n631\t\n632\tOn CS-Dialogue, LLM-P2G is harder to adapt, likely because the available code-switching data are limited and the\n633\tdecoded S2P phoneme sequences remain too noisy for training\n634\ta robust text conversion model. We therefore report only\n635\tprojector-based MER for this dataset. In this setting, Latent\n636\tSoftmax improves both non-tonal PER and MER, achieving a\n637\t9.5% relative MER reduction over standard softmax (15.53 vs.\n638\t14.06). Across both code-switching datasets, Latent Softmax\n639\tconsistently improves projector-based MER, further supporting its advantage for joint Chinese-English training.\n640\tV. A NALYSIS\n641\tA. Effect of S2P encoder improvements in LLM-ASR architectures\n642\tLatent Softmax primarily improves the S2P encoder, so we\n643\tnext examine how these upstream gains affect downstream\n644\tWERs in LLM-ASR architectures. For the LLM-P2G systems in Table I, WER reductions closely follow S2P PER\n645\treductions. Moving from standard softmax to Latent Softmax\n646\treduces PER by 0.17, 0.28, and 0.63 percentage points on\n647\tAISHELL-1, test-clean, and test-other; the first is tonal PER\n648\tand the latter two are non-tonal PERs. The corresponding\n649\tWER reductions are 0.37, 0.38, and 0.81 points. Although the\n650\tWER/PER ratios vary across languages and test sets, the ratios\n651\tare similar for standard softmax and Latent Softmax within\n652\teach evaluation condition. This suggests that the P2G stage\n653\tintroduces a relatively stable conversion effect with respect to\n654\tthe form of symbols emitted from S2P, while improvements\n655\tin S2P quality remain visible at the final WER level.\n656\tFor the projector systems, the same S2P PER improvements\n657\tlead to WER reductions of 0.09, 0.29, and 0.47 points on\n658\tAISHELL-1, test-clean, and test-other. These gains are smaller\n659\tthan those in the LLM-P2G setting. Presumably, this is because the projector interface does not directly rely on a discrete\n660\tphoneme string; it uses continuous representations from the\n661\tS2P encoder before large-language-model decoding. Nevertheless, the consistent WER improvement across all three\n662\tevaluation sets indicates that the upstream S2P representation\n663\tgains from Latent Softmax translate into WER reductions,\n664\trather than merely changing the surface form of decoded\n665\tphoneme symbols.\n666\t\n667\tB. Tonal representation analysis in non-tonal languages\n668\tTable III shows illustrative LibriSpeech examples decoded\n669\tby the Latent Softmax S2P model. The subclass sequences are\n670\tobtained by decoding the subclass posterior distribution. For\n671\tmajor-class decoding, subclass posterior probabilities are first\n672\tmarginalized according to Eq. (5), and beam search is then\n673\tapplied to the resulting major-class distribution.\n674\tThe examples show that, even without explicit English tone\n675\tlabels, the model assigns tone-like subclass marks to English\n676\tvowels. After marginalization, these marks collapse back to\n677\tthe expected major-class phoneme sequence. We treat this\n678\tobservation as qualitative evidence that the latent tone-marked\n679\tvowel subclass space captures prosodic variation useful for\n680\tsharing with tonal-language data. It should not be interpreted\n681\tas a claim that the predicted English tone marks are linguistically ground-truth tones. English still exhibits pitch variation\n682\tin speech production, but such variation is non-contrastive and\n683\ttherefore unmarked in the phonetic transcription. From the\n684\tmodel’s viewpoint, an English vowel token can nevertheless\n685\tinstantiate one latent tone-marked vowel subclass inside the\n686\tcorresponding major class.\n687\tC. Subclass versus major-class phoneme input in LLM-P2G\n688\tTable IV compares LLM-P2G inputs generated from majorclass and subclass S2P outputs. Subclass inputs yield lower\n689\tWER on all three evaluation sets: 5.26% versus 5.31% on\n690\tAISHELL-1, 3.91% versus 4.10% on LibriSpeech test-clean,\n691\tand 9.89% versus 10.19% on test-other. The absolute gains\n692\trange from 0.05 to 0.30 percentage points. This suggests\n693\tthat the subclass representation contains useful fine-grained\n694\tinformation for the P2G model, including in the non-tonal\n695\tEnglish setting. At the same time, the gains are modest, so\n696\tthe result should be understood as supporting evidence rather\n697\tthan a standalone proof of tonal correctness.\n698\tVI. C ONCLUSION\n699\tThis paper studied data-efficient combined training of tonal\n700\tand non-tonal languages for phoneme-based multilingual ASR.\n701\tThe proposed Latent Softmax output layer keeps tone-marked\n702\tvowels as explicit subclasses and uses base vowels as major\n703\tclasses, while keeping consonants and the CTC blank as\n704\tsingleton classes. When only non-tonal base-vowel majorclass labels are available, the tone-marked vowel subclass is\n705\ttreated as latent and marginalized out. This lets non-tonal\n706\tvowel data update a tone-marked vowel subclass space without\n707\terasing tone distinctions, yielding a more data-efficient sharing\n708\tmechanism than standard softmax. Multilingual experiments\n709\ton pooled AISHELL-1 (Madarin) and LibriSpeech (English)\n710\tshow that Latent Softmax consistently improves S2P phoneme\n711\t\n712\t\ferror rate over a standard softmax multilingual baseline and\n713\ttransfers these gains to both LLM-P2G and projector-based\n714\tASR interfaces. Code-switching adaptation on ASRU2019 and\n715\tCS-Dialogue datasets further reduces MER, showing that the\n716\tlearned tone-aware S2P representation remains useful when\n717\tMandarin and English are mixed within utterances. Future\n718\twork can extend the method to more tonal languages and\n719\tlarger multilingual training data, analyze the learned subclass\n720\tassignments quantitatively, and study how latent tone-marked\n721\tvowel subclasses interact with explicit pitch features.\n722\tVII. GENERATIVE AI USE DISCLOSURE\n723\tGenerative AI tools are used in this work only for language\n724\tediting, polishing, and formatting of the manuscript. They\n725\tare not used to generate any core content, research ideas,\n726\texperimental designs, results, or major textual parts of the\n727\tpaper. All scientific contributions, including model design,\n728\texperiments, analysis, and conclusions, are completed by the\n729\tauthors.\n730\tR EFERENCES\n731\t[1] S. Tong, P. N. Garner, and H. Bourlard, “Cross-lingual adaptation of a\n732\tCTC-based multilingual acoustic model,” Speech Communication, vol.\n733\t104, pp. 39–46, 2018.\n734\t[2] S. Yusuyin, H. Huang, J. Liu, and C. Liu, “Investigation into phonebased subword units for multilingual end-to-end speech recognition,” in\n735\tProc. ICASSP, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n736\t[3] S. Yusuyin, T. Ma, H. Huang, W. Zhao, and Z. Ou, “Whistle: Dataefficient multilingual and crosslingual speech recognition via weakly\n737\tphonetic supervision,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and\n738\tLanguage Processing, vol. 33, pp. 1440–1453, 2025.\n739\t[4] S. Yusuyin, T. Ma, H. Huang, and Z. Ou, “Pronunciation-lexicon free\n740\ttraining for phoneme-based crosslingual asr via joint stochastic approximation,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language\n741\tProcessing, vol. 34, pp. 272–284, 2025.\n742\t[5] F. Metze, Z. A. W. Sheikh, A. Waibel, J. Gehring, K. Kilgour, Q. B.\n743\tNguyen, and V. H. Nguyen, “Models of tone for tonal and non-tonal\n744\tlanguages,” in Proc. ASRU, 2013, pp. 261–266.\n745\t[6] H. Lin and Z. Ou, “Switching auxiliary chains for speech recognition,”\n746\tIEEE Signal Processing Letters, vol. 14, no. 8, pp. 568–571, 2007.\n747\t[7] ——, “Switching auxiliary chains for speech recognition based on\n748\tdynamic bayesian networks,” in Proc. International Conference on\n749\tPattern Recognition, 2006, pp. 258–261.\n750\t[8] H. Bu, J. Du, X. Na, B. Wu, and H. Zheng, “AISHELL-1: An opensource mandarin speech corpus and a speech recognition baseline,” in\n751\tProc. O-COCOSDA, 2017, pp. 1–5.\n752\t[9] V. Panayotov, G. Chen, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “LibriSpeech: An\n753\tASR corpus based on public domain audio books,” in Proc. ICASSP,\n754\t2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n755\t[10] T. Ma, M. Bi, S. Yusuyin, H. Huang, and Z. Ou, “LLM-based phonemeto-grapheme for phoneme-based speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2025, pp. 559–563.\n756\t[11] Z. Li, L. Dong, S. Yusuyin, X. Zhao, and Z. Ou, “Phonemes vs.\n757\tprojectors: An investigation of speech-language interfaces for LLMbased ASR,” 2026, arXiv preprint.\n758\t[12] T. Lee, W. Lau, Y. W. Wong, and P. C. Ching, “Using tone information in\n759\tcantonese continuous speech recognition,” ACM Transactions on Asian\n760\tLanguage Information Processing, vol. 1, no. 1, pp. 83–102, 2002.\n761\t[13] X. Lei, M.-H. Siu, M. J. F. Gales, M.-Y. Hwang, and P. C. Woodland,\n762\t“Improved tone modeling for mandarin broadcast news speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2006, pp. 1237–1240.\n763\t[14] J. Li and M. Hasegawa-Johnson, “Autosegmental neural nets: Should\n764\tphones and tones be synchronous or asynchronous?” in Proc. Interspeech, 2020, pp. 1027–1031.\n765\t[15] C. Zhu, K. An, H. Zheng, and Z. Ou, “Multilingual and crosslingual\n766\tspeech recognition using phonological-vector based phone embeddings,”\n767\tin Proc. ASRU, 2021, pp. 1034–1041.\n768\t\n769\t[16] X. Li, S. Dalmia, J. Li, M. Lee, P. Littell, J. Yao, A. Anastasopoulos,\n770\tD. R. Mortensen, G. Neubig, A. W. Black, and F. Metze, “Universal\n771\tphone recognition with a multilingual allophone system,” in Proc.\n772\tICASSP, 2020, pp. 8249–8253.\n773\t[17] J. Chen, P. Wang, J. Liu, and Y. Qian, “Label relation graphs enhanced\n774\thierarchical residual network for hierarchical multi-granularity classification,” in Proc. CVPR, 2022, pp. 4858–4867.\n775\t[18] Y. Xu, Q. Qian, H. Li, R. Jin, and J. Hu, “Weakly supervised representation learning with coarse labels,” in Proc. ICCV, 2021.\n776\t[19] J. Lv, M. Xu, L. Feng, G. Niu, X. Geng, and M. Sugiyama, “Progressive\n777\tidentification of true labels for partial-label learning,” in Proceedings of\n778\tthe 37th International Conference on Machine Learning, ser. Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, vol. 119, 2020.\n779\t[20] Z. Ou, “A review of learning with deep generative models from\n780\tperspective of graphical modeling,” 2018.\n781\t[21] X. Shi, Q. Feng, and L. Xie, “The ASRU 2019 Mandarin-English codeswitching speech recognition challenge: Open datasets, tracks, methods\n782\tand results,” 2020.\n783\t[22] J. Zhou, Y. Guo, S. Zhao, H. Sun, H. Wang, J. He, A. Kong, S. Wang,\n784\tX. Yang, Y. Wang, Y. Lin, and Y. Qin, “CS-Dialogue: A 104-hour dataset\n785\tof spontaneous Mandarin-English code-switching dialogues for speech\n786\trecognition,” 2025.\n787\t\n788\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4fb4612c-13e8-4aee-b47f-67a2a98fb31a", "uuid": "83141a84-92eb-4b84-af1a-c00f0ba34efe", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T16:51:41.490Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "Latent Softmax for Data-Efficient Phoneme-Based\nMultilingual ASR Across Tonal and Non-Tonal\nLanguages\nSaierdaer Yusuyin1 † , Nanling Jiang2 † , Hao Huang1 , Zhijian Ou3,4 *\n1\n\narXiv:2608.01281v1 [eess.AS] 2 Aug 2026\n\n2\n\nSchool of Computer Science and Technology, Xinjiang University, China\nDepartment of Electronic Engineering and Information Science, University of Science and Technology of China, China\n3\nSpeech Processing and Machine Intelligence (SPMI) Lab, Tsinghua University, China\n4\nTasiTech Co. Ltd., China\nsar_dar@foxmail.com, huanghao@xju.edu.cn, ozj@tsinghua.edu.cn\n\nAbstract—Phoneme-based multilingual automatic speech\nrecognition (ASR) can share acoustic evidence across languages\nmore directly than language-specific subword modeling. When\ntonal and non-tonal languages are jointly trained, however, their\nsupervision granularity does not match: tonal languages annotate tone-marked vowels, whereas non-tonal languages typically\nprovide only base-vowel labels. A standard softmax either treats\nthe two as unrelated classes, weakening cross-lingual sharing, or\ncollapses tones, losing distinctions required by tonal languages.\nWe propose Latent Softmax, a connectionist temporal classification (CTC)-compatible output layer that models tone-marked\nvowels as subclasses and base vowels as major classes, while\nconsonants and the CTC blank remain singleton labels. When\nonly a base-vowel major-class label is observed, the tone-marked\nvowel subclass is treated as latent and marginalized out. Multilingual experiments on AISHELL-1 Mandarin and LibriSpeech\nEnglish show that Latent Softmax reduces speech-to-phoneme\n(S2P) phoneme error rates over a standard softmax multilingual baseline by 8.4% on AISHELL-1, 17.5% on LibriSpeech\ntest-clean, and 12.6% on test-other. The improved speech-tophoneme encoders also yield consistent word error rate gains\nfor both large-language-model phoneme-to-grapheme conversion\nand projector-based interfaces. After code-switching adaptation,\nLatent Softmax further reduces projector-based mixed error rate\nby 2.6% on ASRU2019 and 9.5% on CS-Dialogue datasets.\nIndex Terms—multilingual ASR, phoneme-based ASR, tone\nmodeling, Latent Softmax, CTC\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nMultilingual and cross-lingual ASR are attractive because\ndata from multiple languages can be pooled to improve\ntraining efficiency, especially in low-resource settings. Recent\nphoneme-based multilingual ASR has renewed this idea by\nusing phonetic supervision as a language-agnostic interface.\nCompared with language-specific subword units, phonemebased training encourages finer acoustic-phonetic sharing, and\nrecent work reports advantages in multilingual recognition,\ncross-lingual transfer, and data efficiency [1]–[4]. Weakly\nphonetic systems such as Whistle further show that phonetic\nsupervision can be more data-efficient than subword supervision in multilingual and cross-lingual ASR [3].\n* Corresponding author.\n† Work done during internship at TasiTech.\n\nFor multilingual training involving both tonal and non-tonal\nlanguages, a straightforward solution is to place all symbols\nin one output inventory and treat a tone-marked vowel and\nits non-tonal base-vowel counterpart as separate units. For\nexample, Mandarin /ā/ and English /a/ would occupy different\nsoftmax outputs. This preserves Mandarin tone distinctions,\nbut it limits direct sharing: English /a/ frames do not directly\nprovide positive supervision to Mandarin /ā/, /á/, /ǎ/, and /à/,\nand vice versa. The opposite strategy, collapsing all tonemarked vowel variants into the same base vowel, improves\nsharing but removes distinctions that are essential for tonal\nlanguages. The resulting research question is: is there a dataefficient mechanism for combined training of tonal and nontonal languages? Equivalently, how should tones be incorporated in multilingual phoneme-based models?\nThe core challenge is that non-tonal vowel supervision\nprovides only base-vowel major-class labels while the tonal\nlanguage requires a finer tone-marked-vowel subclass space.\nAt the same time, non-tonal speech is not pitch-free: it still\nexhibits pitch variation in production, but such variation is linguistically non-contrastive and therefore unmarked in phonetic\ntranscription [5]–[7]. This suggests a latent-view interpretation\nof non-tonal supervision: an English vowel token can be\nregarded as an acoustic manifestation of one member of a\ntone-marked vowel subclass family, even though that subclass\nidentity is not annotated.\nTo address this challenge, we propose Latent Softmax.\nSoftmax is computed over subclasses. Base vowels are major\nclasses; when only a base-vowel label is observed, the tonemarked vowel subclass is latent and marginalized. Tonemarked vowel labels remain explicit subclasses for tonal\nlanguages. For non-singleton vowel groups, major classes have\nno independent output neurons and are modeled by sums of\nsubclass probabilities. Figure 1 illustrates the idea.\nLatent Softmax enables more efficient sharing than standard\nsoftmax in joint tonal/non-tonal training. For example, Mandarin /ā/ and English /a/ data can reinforce each other through\na shared base-vowel hierarchy: English /a/ spreads posterior\nmass across compatible tone-marked vowel subclasses, while\n\n\fMandarin /ā/ sharpens one subclass. Thus, non-tonal vowels\nimplicitly augment the tone-marked subclass space. Bilingual\nexperiments on AISHELL-1 Mandarin [8] and LibriSpeech\nEnglish [9] show improvements in both speech-to-phoneme\n(S2P) and downstream word recognition with large-languagemodel phoneme-to-grapheme (LLM-P2G) conversion [10] and\nprojector-based interfaces [11]. After code-switching adaptation, Latent Softmax further reduces mixed error rates on\nASRU2019 and CS-Dialogue datasets.\nII. R ELATED W ORK\nA. Tone modeling in ASR\nTone modeling is well studied for tonal-language ASR.\nIn Chinese ASR, tone-marked vowels are commonly used\nin acoustic modeling, often with fundamental-frequency (F0)\nfeatures appended to frame-level spectral features [12], [13].\nRecent CTC work revisits joint versus separate phone-tone\nmodeling [14]; synchronous models using consonants and\ntone-marked vowels achieve lower error rate on the joint\nphone+tone tier [14]. These studies address tonal-language\nrepresentations, while we target mixed tonal/non-tonal training\nwhere non-tonal data have no tone labels.\nTone-related representations have also been studied for nontonal languages [5]–[7], suggesting that pitch and tone-related\ncues can improve non-tonal ASR. Latent Softmax instead\npromotes tonal/non-tonal sharing by marginalizing over tonemarked vowel subclasses when only base-vowel labels are\navailable, without adding tone features or predicting tone for\nevery language.\nB. Multilingual training involving both tonal and non-tonal\nlanguages\nPhoneme-based multilingual ASR commonly uses a shared\ninventory, often based on the International Phonetic Alphabet\n(IPA), to encourage cross-lingual sharing [1]–[4]. Weakly\nphonetic systems such as Whistle show that IPA-based supervision can be more data-efficient than subword supervision,\nbut mainly study non-tonal languages and identify tone incorporation as open [3].\nSome related work involves tonal and non-tonal languages\nbut addresses the problem differently. JoinAP links phone\nembeddings via phonological features and evaluates CommonVoice European multilingual training with zero-shot and\nfew-shot transfer to AISHELL-1 Mandarin [15]. Universal\nphone recognition with a multilingual allophone system models language-dependent phonemes with language-independent\nphones [16]. These approaches are related in spirit, but do not\ndirectly address the supervision mismatch where tonal languages provide tone-marked vowels and non-tonal languages\nprovide only base vowels.\nMore generally, simply concatenating tone-marked and nontonal vowel units preserves discriminability but limits sharing\nbetween base vowels and tonal variants. Latent Softmax fills\nthis gap by using each base vowel as a major class over tonemarked subclasses, letting non-tonal base-vowel labels train\nthe subclass space without tone annotations.\n\nC. Relation to hierarchical classification and partial supervision\nLatent Softmax is also related to mixed-granularity or partial\nsupervision outside speech. In hierarchical multi-granularity\nclassification, a sample may have coarse or fine labels, and\nrecent losses maximize the observed label’s marginal probability by aggregating compatible descendants [17]. Coarselabel representation learning [18] and partial-label learning\n[19] likewise treat supervision as a set of candidate fine labels.\nOur formulation is closest to this marginalization view.\nHowever, these formulations are not designed for CTC\nsequence modeling or multilingual phoneme spaces mixing\ntonal and non-tonal supervision. We introduce latent subclass\nmarginalization into tonal/non-tonal multilingual S2P modeling and show that it improves data sharing in multilingual\nASR.\nIII. M ETHOD\nA. Standard softmax for CTC-based S2P\nLet L denote the label inventory used by a standard speechto-phoneme (S2P) model, including phoneme labels and the\nCTC blank. Given acoustic input x, a standard softmax layer\nproduces logits ut (l) for each l ∈ L and frame posterior\nexp(ut (l))\n.\n′\nl′ ∈L exp(ut (l ))\n\npt (l) = p(πt = l | x) = P\n\n(1)\n\nFor target label sequence y, the CTC likelihood is\nP (y | x) =\n\nT\nX Y\n\npt (πt ),\n\n(2)\n\nπ∈A(y) t=1\n\nwhere A(y) is the set of frame-level alignments that collapse\nto y.\nConsider phoneme-based multilingual training involving\nboth tonal and non-tonal languages. Under standard softmax,\na Mandarin tone-marked vowel label such as /ā/ and an\nEnglish base-vowel label such as /a/ are either treated as\nseparate classes or manually collapsed. The former reduces\ndata sharing; the latter discards tone distinctions. Latent Softmax changes only the output-probability computation, while\nkeeping the CTC objective in Eq. (2) unchanged in form.\nB. Latent Softmax formulation\nLet Z be the subclass inventory. The softmax computation is\nover subclasses. These subclasses include tone-marked vowels\nfor tonal languages, together with singleton labels that do not\nneed further subdivision, including consonants and the CTC\nblank. For vowels, the corresponding base vowel defines a major class; for example, /ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/ belong to the major class\n/a/. Consonants and the CTC blank remain singleton labels\nand do not participate in this major/subclass decomposition.\nEach training utterance comes with a target sequence y. We\nuse the term supervised label for an element of this sequence,\nnamely the label actually provided by the training corpus after\nphoneme conversion. For a Mandarin utterance, a supervised\nlabel may be a tone-marked vowel such as /ā/. For an English\n\n\fFig. 1. Overview of standard softmax and Latent Softmax for combined training of tonal and non-tonal languages. Standard softmax treats non-tonal base\nvowels and tone-marked vowels as independent output classes. Latent Softmax keeps tone-marked vowels as subclasses. A tone-labeled sample supervises\na singleton tone-marked vowel subclass, while a non-tonal base-vowel sample supervises the base-vowel major class obtained by marginalizing compatible\ntone-marked vowel subclasses; in this case the subclass identity is latent.\n\nutterance, a supervised label may instead be a base vowel\nsuch as /a/. For each supervised label l, define the compatiblesubclass set\nB(l) = {z ∈ Z : z is compatible with l}.\n\n(3)\n\nIf l is a non-tonal base vowel, then B(l) contains all tonemarked vowel subclasses of that base vowel; otherwise,\nB(l) = {l}. The second case covers tone-marked vowels as\nwell as singleton labels such as consonants and blank.\nThe network outputs subclass logits vt (z) and the subclass\nposterior is\nexp(vt (z))\n.\n′\nz ′ ∈Z exp(vt (z ))\n\npt (z) = p(zt = z | x) = P\n\nThe frame-level probability used by CTC is then\nX\nqt (l) =\npt (z).\n\n(4)\n\n(5)\n\nz∈B(l)\n\nThus, an English base-vowel label /a/ uses B(/a/) =\n{/ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/}, while a Mandarin tone-marked vowel label /ā/\nuses B(/ā/) = {/ā/}, assuming four Mandarin tone-marked\nvowel subclasses. In the phonetic representation used here,\ntones are attached to the central vowel in a syllable. This is\na practical transcription choice: tones are suprasegmental and\ncan extend beyond the vowel segment boundary, but they are\nconventionally marked on the syllabic nucleus because their\nmost salient effect is typically on the central vowel.\nThe corresponding CTC likelihood becomes\nP (y | x) =\n\nX\n\nT\nY\n\nC. Gradient interpretation for Latent Softmax\nFor analysis, define the aggregated logit of supervised label\nl as\nX\nut (l) = log\nexp(vt (z ′ )).\n(7)\nz ′ ∈B(l)\n\nThen the frame-level probability used by CTC can be rewritten as\nexp(ut (l))\n.\n(8)\nqt (l) = P\n′\n′\nz ∈Z exp(vt (z ))\nHere ut (l) is only a convenient aggregated logit used for\ninterpretation; Latent Softmax does not introduce separate\noutput neurons for major classes.\nThe neural network outputs logits over subclasses. Consider\nthe gradient of the log likelihood with respect to the logit\nfor subclass z. The gradients with respect to the network\nparameters can then be obtained from these logit gradients\nby standard back-propagation. Let γt (l) = P (πt = l | x, y)\nbe the CTC posterior of label l at frame t, obtained by the\nforward-backward algorithm; and note that the same subclass\nz may occur several times in a single utterance, i.e., there can\nbe multiple supervised-label occurrences l in an utterance such\nthat z ∈ B(l). Then\n∂ log P (y | x) X\n∂ log qt (l)\n=\nγt (l)\n∂vt (z)\n∂vt (z)\nl\nX\n∂ut (l)\n=\nγt (l)\n− pt (z)\n∂vt (z)\nl:z∈B(l)\n\n=\n\nX\nl:z∈B(l)\n\nqt (πt ).\n\n(6)\n\nπ∈A(y) t=1\n\nThus, Latent Softmax can be implemented as a deterministic\naggregation layer between the subclass softmax and the CTC\ncriterion. Once the aggregated probabilities qt (l) are formed, a\nstandard CTC forward-backward implementation can evaluate\nthe loss, and automatic differentiation in toolkits such as PyTorch can backpropagate gradients to the network parameters.\n\nγt (l)\n\npt (z)\n− pt (z).\nqt (l)\n\n(9)\n\nThe same result can also be obtained from a latent-subclass\nview. Let zt be the latent subclass at frame t, and let z =\nz1 , . . . , zT . By Fisher’s identity [20],\n\u0015\n\u0014\n∂\n∂\nlog P (y | x) = EP (z|x,y)\nlog pt (zt )\n∂vt (z)\n∂vt (z)\n= EP (z|x,y) [δ(zt = z) − pt (z)]\n= P (zt = z | x, y) − pt (z),\n\n(10)\n\n\fFig. 2. Phoneme-count distribution in LibriSpeech English and AISHELL-1 Mandarin. The horizontal axis lists English and Mandarin phoneme labels, ordered\nby descending major-class count, and the vertical axis reports their corpus-level counts in the two datasets. When a phoneme appears in both English and\nChinese, two bars are shown side-by-side. Base vowels and their tone-marked vowel subclasses, such as /a/, /ā/, /á/, /ǎ/, and /à/, are placed contiguously to\nshow how non-tonal and tonal evidence is distributed across the shared inventory.\n\nwhere the posterior assigned to subclass z is\nP (zt = z | x, y)\n(11)\nX\n=\nP (πt = l | x, y)P (zt = z | x, y, πt = l)\nl:z∈B(l)\n\n=\n\nX\nl:z∈B(l)\n\nγt (l)\n\npt (z)\n.\nqt (l)\n\n(12)\n\nThus Eq. (9) and Eq. (10) coincide.\nThe two derivations emphasize complementary aspects of\nthe method. The chain-rule view shows how the positive CTC\nposterior mass of each supervised label is redistributed across\nall compatible subclasses. The Fisher-identity view shows\nthe probabilistic meaning: when only a base-vowel label is\nobserved, the tone-marked vowel subclass is latent and its\nposterior is inferred rather than fixed. Note that for labels\nother than non-tonal base vowels, B(l) is a singleton and the\ngradient reduces to the usual CTC-softmax form.\nThis also gives a direct data-sharing interpretation. For\na non-tonal base-vowel label, the positive posterior mass is\ndistributed across all compatible tone-marked vowel subclasses\naccording to the current model belief. Therefore, non-tonal\nvowel frames update the tone-marked vowel subclass space\nwithout requiring tone annotations. In a standard softmax,\nMandarin /ā/ and English /a/ are separate output classes. In\nLatent Softmax, English /a/ contributes posterior mass to the\ncompatible subclass family {/ā/,/á/,/ǎ/,/à/}, while Mandarin /ā/\nsharpens one specific subclass inside the same major class.\nThe two sources of data therefore mutually augment each other\nthrough the shared base-vowel hierarchy.\nD. Evaluation metrics\nWe report tonal phoneme error rate (T-PER), non-tonal\nphoneme error rate (NT-PER), word error rate (WER), and\nmixed error rate (MER). Both tonal PER and non-tonal PER\nare edit distances between decoded and reference phoneme\nsequences, normalized by the reference phoneme length. Tonal\nPER is computed on tone-marked phoneme sequences and is\nused for the Mandarin AISHELL-1 test set. Non-tonal PER\nis computed after removing tone marks from the decoded\nand reference phoneme sequences; it is used for the English\n\nLibriSpeech test sets and for the Mandarin–English codeswitching test sets. This distinction is needed because the\nLatent Softmax S2P model can decode tone-marked vowel\nsubclasses even for English, while the English reference\nphoneme sequence has no tone labels. WER is computed as the\nnormalized edit distance between decoded and reference word\nsequences. For Mandarin–English code-switching evaluation,\nwe use MER, where Mandarin characters and English words\nare treated as mixed tokens for edit-distance scoring.\nIV. E XPERIMENTS\nA. Datasets and setup\nWe conduct multilingual experiments by pooling data from\none tonal language (Mandarin) and one non-tonal language\n(English). AISHELL-1 is used as the Mandarin data, containing approximately 178 hours of read speech from 400 speakers\n[8]. LibriSpeech is used as the English data; it contains\napproximately 1000 hours of 16 kHz read speech, and we\nreport results on the official test-clean and test-other subsets\n[9]. Text transcripts are converted to phoneme sequences using\nthe same lexicon or grapheme-to-phoneme pipeline for all\ncompared S2P systems.\nWe further evaluate code-switching adaptation on two\nMandarin–English code-switching datasets. The ASRU2019\nMandarin-English code-switching challenge data contain 500\nhours of Mandarin-only speech, a 200-hour Train_CS set, a\n40-hour Dev_CS set, and a 20.6-hour Test_CS set [21]. In\nour code-switching fine-tuning experiments, the additional 500\nhours of Mandarin-only speech are not used. CS-Dialogue is\na spontaneous Mandarin-English dialogue corpus with 104.02\nhours of speech from 200 speakers and 38,917 utterances\n[22]. It provides speaker-independent training, development,\nand test splits of 68.97, 18.30, and 16.74 hours, respectively.\nFigure 2 visualizes the empirical label distribution behind\nthe mixed English–Mandarin training setup. The figure makes\nexplicit the supervision mismatch addressed by Latent Softmax: one base vowel can appear as a non-tonal English\nlabel and as multiple Mandarin tone-marked vowel subclasses.\nUnder a standard softmax these counts are consumed by\nseparate output classes, whereas Latent Softmax lets non-tonal\nbase-vowel observations train the corresponding base-vowel\n\n\fTABLE I\nT ONAL PER S (%), NON - TONAL PER S (%), AND WER S (%) ON AISHELL-1 AND L IBRI S PEECH . AISHELL-1 USES TONAL PER, WHILE L IBRI S PEECH\nUSES NON - TONAL PER. PER IS MEASURED AT THE S2P OUTPUT.\nAISHELL-1\nSystem\n\nLibriSpeech\ntest-clean\ntest-other\nNon-tonal PER\nWER\nNon-tonal PER\nWER\n\nTonal PER\n\nWER\n\nStandard Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\nStandard Softmax S2P + Projector\n\n2.03\n\n5.63\n5.61\n\n1.60\n\n4.29\n3.92\n\n5.00\n\n10.70\n9.61\n\nLatent Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\nLatent Softmax S2P + Projector\n\n1.86\n\n5.26\n5.52\n\n1.32\n\n3.91\n3.63\n\n4.37\n\n9.89\n9.14\n\nTABLE II\nN ON - TONAL (NT) PER S (%) AND MER S (%) ON M ANDARIN –E NGLISH\nCODE - SWITCHING DATASETS AFTER CODE - SWITCHING ADAPTATION .\nSystem\n\nASRU2019\nNTMER\nPER\n\nCS-Dialogue\nNTMER\nPER\n\nStandard Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\nStandard Softmax S2P + Projector\n\n3.23\n\n13.69\n14.08\n\n9.66\n\n15.53\n\nLatent Softmax S2P + LLM-P2G\nLatent Softmax S2P + Projector\n\n3.83\n\n13.83\n13.71\n\n9.02\n\n14.06\n\nmajor class and, through marginalization, the associated tonemarked vowel subclass family.\nThe S2P encoder follows a Conformer-based configuration\ncomparable to the Whistle-small setting [3]. We use Qwen31.7B as the LLM. LoRA uses rank r = 1024 and LoRA alpha\nα = 2048. The projector is a two-layer 4096-dimensional\nlinear projection with a ReLU activation between the two\nlinear layers. We compare a standard softmax multilingual\nS2P baseline and the proposed Latent Softmax S2P model.\nThe standard softmax baseline treats the multilingual output\ninventory as flat labels. Latent Softmax uses the same encoder\narchitecture but replaces the flat output probability with the\nmarginalization layer in Eq. (5). Downstream word recognition\nis evaluated with two speech-language interfaces: an LLMP2G system that converts S2P phoneme sequences to text [10],\nand a projector-based system that maps S2P representations to\nlarge-language-model token embeddings [11].\nIn the main experiments, the LLM-P2G interface trains the\nS2P model and the LLM-P2G model in two separate stages.\nThe projector interface adopts a three-stage pipeline: (1) S2P\nis trained first; (2) after removing the S2P linear classifier,\nthe projector is trained while the S2P encoder and LLM are\nfrozen; (3) finally, the LLM LoRA parameters are trained with\nboth the S2P encoder and projector fixed.\nCode-switching adaptation reuses the same stage-wise protocol. All modules are initialized from the main experiment,\nand the projector is kept frozen throughout. For the projector\ninterface, this means fine-tuning S2P first and then the LLM\nLoRA parameters. For the LLM-P2G interface, S2P and LLMP2G are fine-tuned separately in the same order.\nB. Main results\nTable I summarizes the main results. Latent Softmax reduces S2P PER from 2.03% to 1.86% on AISHELL-1, from\n1.60% to 1.32% on LibriSpeech test-clean, and from 5.00%\nto 4.37% on test-other. These correspond to relative PER\n\nreductions of 8.4%, 17.5%, and 12.6%, respectively, over\nthe standard softmax multilingual baseline. The AISHELL-1\nnumber is tonal PER, and the LibriSpeech numbers are nontonal PERs.\nThe downstream WERs show the same general trend. With\nLLM-P2G, Latent Softmax improves WER from 5.63% to\n5.26% on AISHELL-1, from 4.29% to 3.91% on test-clean,\nand from 10.70% to 9.89% on test-other. With the projector\ninterface, it improves WER from 5.61% to 5.52%, from\n3.92% to 3.63%, and from 9.61% to 9.14%. The largest WER\ngains occur in the LLM-P2G setting, where the phoneme\nsequence emitted by S2P directly determines the P2G input.\nThe projector interface is already strong on LibriSpeech, so\nthe absolute gains are smaller, but Latent Softmax remains\nconsistently better than the corresponding standard softmax\ncounterpart.\nIt is important not to overstate a single downstream interface\nas universally best. Within the Latent Softmax systems, LLMP2G gives the lowest WER on AISHELL-1 (5.26%), whereas\nthe projector interface gives lower WERs on LibriSpeech\n(3.63% on test-clean and 9.14% on test-other). The main\nconclusion from Table I is therefore not that one interface\ndominates, but that Latent Softmax improves the shared S2P\nencoder and that the improvement transfers to both downstream ASR interfaces.\nC. Fine-tuning on Code-switching Datasets\nThe AISHELL-1 and LibriSpeech evaluations above pool\nMandarin and English data, but each utterance is singlelanguage rather than code-switched. We therefore further\nconsider Mandarin–English code-switching, a more difficult\nsetting where languages may mix within an utterance. Table II reports adaptation results obtained by fine-tuning the\ncorresponding systems in Table I. We evaluate both LLM-P2G\nand projector interfaces on ASRU2019. For CS-Dialogue, the\nLLM-P2G training was unstable and is therefore omitted, as\ndiscussed below.\nOn ASRU2019, the two downstream interfaces behave\ndifferently. With LLM-P2G, standard softmax S2P gives a\nslightly lower MER than Latent Softmax (13.69 vs. 13.83),\nconsistent with its lower non-tonal PER (3.23 vs. 3.83).\nWith the projector interface, however, Latent Softmax reduces\nMER by 2.6% relative to standard softmax (14.08 vs. 13.71),\nsuggesting that the projector approach still benefits from the\ntone-aware representation learned by Latent Softmax.\n\n\fTABLE III\nE XAMPLE SUBCLASS AND MAJOR - CLASS PHONEME DECODING ON L IBRI S PEECH . S UBCLASS SEQUENCES ARE DECODED FROM SUBCLASS POSTERIORS ;\nMAJOR - CLASS SEQUENCES ARE DECODED AFTER MARGINALIZING SUBCLASS POSTERIOR PROBABILITIES .\ntext\n\nsubclass phoneme sequence\n\nmajor class phoneme sequence\n\nwhere is that\nyes i know very well\nwhat made the difference\n\nw è õ í z ð æ th\nj è s á í n ó Ú v @` õ í w è l\nw @` th m è ı̄ d ð @ˇ d ì f @ˇ õ @ˇ n s\n\nw e õ i z ð æ th\njesainoUv@õiwel\nw @ th m e i d ð @ d i f @ õ @ n s\n\nTABLE IV\nWER S (%) OF THE LLM-P2G MODEL WHEN USING SUBCLASS AND\nMAJOR - CLASS S2P PHONEME SEQUENCES AS INPUT, RESPECTIVELY.\nS2P output\n\nAISHELL\n\nLS clean\n\nLS other\n\nMajor class\nSubclass\n\n5.31\n5.26\n\n4.10\n3.91\n\n10.19\n9.89\n\nOn CS-Dialogue, LLM-P2G is harder to adapt, likely because the available code-switching data are limited and the\ndecoded S2P phoneme sequences remain too noisy for training\na robust text conversion model. We therefore report only\nprojector-based MER for this dataset. In this setting, Latent\nSoftmax improves both non-tonal PER and MER, achieving a\n9.5% relative MER reduction over standard softmax (15.53 vs.\n14.06). Across both code-switching datasets, Latent Softmax\nconsistently improves projector-based MER, further supporting its advantage for joint Chinese-English training.\nV. A NALYSIS\nA. Effect of S2P encoder improvements in LLM-ASR architectures\nLatent Softmax primarily improves the S2P encoder, so we\nnext examine how these upstream gains affect downstream\nWERs in LLM-ASR architectures. For the LLM-P2G systems in Table I, WER reductions closely follow S2P PER\nreductions. Moving from standard softmax to Latent Softmax\nreduces PER by 0.17, 0.28, and 0.63 percentage points on\nAISHELL-1, test-clean, and test-other; the first is tonal PER\nand the latter two are non-tonal PERs. The corresponding\nWER reductions are 0.37, 0.38, and 0.81 points. Although the\nWER/PER ratios vary across languages and test sets, the ratios\nare similar for standard softmax and Latent Softmax within\neach evaluation condition. This suggests that the P2G stage\nintroduces a relatively stable conversion effect with respect to\nthe form of symbols emitted from S2P, while improvements\nin S2P quality remain visible at the final WER level.\nFor the projector systems, the same S2P PER improvements\nlead to WER reductions of 0.09, 0.29, and 0.47 points on\nAISHELL-1, test-clean, and test-other. These gains are smaller\nthan those in the LLM-P2G setting. Presumably, this is because the projector interface does not directly rely on a discrete\nphoneme string; it uses continuous representations from the\nS2P encoder before large-language-model decoding. Nevertheless, the consistent WER improvement across all three\nevaluation sets indicates that the upstream S2P representation\ngains from Latent Softmax translate into WER reductions,\nrather than merely changing the surface form of decoded\nphoneme symbols.\n\nB. Tonal representation analysis in non-tonal languages\nTable III shows illustrative LibriSpeech examples decoded\nby the Latent Softmax S2P model. The subclass sequences are\nobtained by decoding the subclass posterior distribution. For\nmajor-class decoding, subclass posterior probabilities are first\nmarginalized according to Eq. (5), and beam search is then\napplied to the resulting major-class distribution.\nThe examples show that, even without explicit English tone\nlabels, the model assigns tone-like subclass marks to English\nvowels. After marginalization, these marks collapse back to\nthe expected major-class phoneme sequence. We treat this\nobservation as qualitative evidence that the latent tone-marked\nvowel subclass space captures prosodic variation useful for\nsharing with tonal-language data. It should not be interpreted\nas a claim that the predicted English tone marks are linguistically ground-truth tones. English still exhibits pitch variation\nin speech production, but such variation is non-contrastive and\ntherefore unmarked in the phonetic transcription. From the\nmodel’s viewpoint, an English vowel token can nevertheless\ninstantiate one latent tone-marked vowel subclass inside the\ncorresponding major class.\nC. Subclass versus major-class phoneme input in LLM-P2G\nTable IV compares LLM-P2G inputs generated from majorclass and subclass S2P outputs. Subclass inputs yield lower\nWER on all three evaluation sets: 5.26% versus 5.31% on\nAISHELL-1, 3.91% versus 4.10% on LibriSpeech test-clean,\nand 9.89% versus 10.19% on test-other. The absolute gains\nrange from 0.05 to 0.30 percentage points. This suggests\nthat the subclass representation contains useful fine-grained\ninformation for the P2G model, including in the non-tonal\nEnglish setting. At the same time, the gains are modest, so\nthe result should be understood as supporting evidence rather\nthan a standalone proof of tonal correctness.\nVI. C ONCLUSION\nThis paper studied data-efficient combined training of tonal\nand non-tonal languages for phoneme-based multilingual ASR.\nThe proposed Latent Softmax output layer keeps tone-marked\nvowels as explicit subclasses and uses base vowels as major\nclasses, while keeping consonants and the CTC blank as\nsingleton classes. When only non-tonal base-vowel majorclass labels are available, the tone-marked vowel subclass is\ntreated as latent and marginalized out. This lets non-tonal\nvowel data update a tone-marked vowel subclass space without\nerasing tone distinctions, yielding a more data-efficient sharing\nmechanism than standard softmax. Multilingual experiments\non pooled AISHELL-1 (Madarin) and LibriSpeech (English)\nshow that Latent Softmax consistently improves S2P phoneme\n\n\ferror rate over a standard softmax multilingual baseline and\ntransfers these gains to both LLM-P2G and projector-based\nASR interfaces. Code-switching adaptation on ASRU2019 and\nCS-Dialogue datasets further reduces MER, showing that the\nlearned tone-aware S2P representation remains useful when\nMandarin and English are mixed within utterances. Future\nwork can extend the method to more tonal languages and\nlarger multilingual training data, analyze the learned subclass\nassignments quantitatively, and study how latent tone-marked\nvowel subclasses interact with explicit pitch features.\nVII. GENERATIVE AI USE DISCLOSURE\nGenerative AI tools are used in this work only for language\nediting, polishing, and formatting of the manuscript. They\nare not used to generate any core content, research ideas,\nexperimental designs, results, or major textual parts of the\npaper. All scientific contributions, including model design,\nexperiments, analysis, and conclusions, are completed by the\nauthors.\nR EFERENCES\n[1] S. Tong, P. N. Garner, and H. Bourlard, “Cross-lingual adaptation of a\nCTC-based multilingual acoustic model,” Speech Communication, vol.\n104, pp. 39–46, 2018.\n[2] S. Yusuyin, H. Huang, J. Liu, and C. Liu, “Investigation into phonebased subword units for multilingual end-to-end speech recognition,” in\nProc. ICASSP, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n[3] S. Yusuyin, T. Ma, H. Huang, W. Zhao, and Z. Ou, “Whistle: Dataefficient multilingual and crosslingual speech recognition via weakly\nphonetic supervision,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and\nLanguage Processing, vol. 33, pp. 1440–1453, 2025.\n[4] S. Yusuyin, T. Ma, H. Huang, and Z. Ou, “Pronunciation-lexicon free\ntraining for phoneme-based crosslingual asr via joint stochastic approximation,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language\nProcessing, vol. 34, pp. 272–284, 2025.\n[5] F. Metze, Z. A. W. Sheikh, A. Waibel, J. Gehring, K. Kilgour, Q. B.\nNguyen, and V. H. Nguyen, “Models of tone for tonal and non-tonal\nlanguages,” in Proc. ASRU, 2013, pp. 261–266.\n[6] H. Lin and Z. Ou, “Switching auxiliary chains for speech recognition,”\nIEEE Signal Processing Letters, vol. 14, no. 8, pp. 568–571, 2007.\n[7] ——, “Switching auxiliary chains for speech recognition based on\ndynamic bayesian networks,” in Proc. International Conference on\nPattern Recognition, 2006, pp. 258–261.\n[8] H. Bu, J. Du, X. Na, B. Wu, and H. Zheng, “AISHELL-1: An opensource mandarin speech corpus and a speech recognition baseline,” in\nProc. O-COCOSDA, 2017, pp. 1–5.\n[9] V. Panayotov, G. Chen, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “LibriSpeech: An\nASR corpus based on public domain audio books,” in Proc. ICASSP,\n2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n[10] T. Ma, M. Bi, S. Yusuyin, H. Huang, and Z. Ou, “LLM-based phonemeto-grapheme for phoneme-based speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2025, pp. 559–563.\n[11] Z. Li, L. Dong, S. Yusuyin, X. Zhao, and Z. Ou, “Phonemes vs.\nprojectors: An investigation of speech-language interfaces for LLMbased ASR,” 2026, arXiv preprint.\n[12] T. Lee, W. Lau, Y. W. Wong, and P. C. Ching, “Using tone information in\ncantonese continuous speech recognition,” ACM Transactions on Asian\nLanguage Information Processing, vol. 1, no. 1, pp. 83–102, 2002.\n[13] X. Lei, M.-H. Siu, M. J. F. Gales, M.-Y. Hwang, and P. C. Woodland,\n“Improved tone modeling for mandarin broadcast news speech recognition,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2006, pp. 1237–1240.\n[14] J. Li and M. Hasegawa-Johnson, “Autosegmental neural nets: Should\nphones and tones be synchronous or asynchronous?” in Proc. Interspeech, 2020, pp. 1027–1031.\n[15] C. Zhu, K. An, H. Zheng, and Z. Ou, “Multilingual and crosslingual\nspeech recognition using phonological-vector based phone embeddings,”\nin Proc. ASRU, 2021, pp. 1034–1041.\n\n[16] X. Li, S. Dalmia, J. Li, M. Lee, P. Littell, J. Yao, A. Anastasopoulos,\nD. R. Mortensen, G. Neubig, A. W. Black, and F. Metze, “Universal\nphone recognition with a multilingual allophone system,” in Proc.\nICASSP, 2020, pp. 8249–8253.\n[17] J. Chen, P. Wang, J. Liu, and Y. Qian, “Label relation graphs enhanced\nhierarchical residual network for hierarchical multi-granularity classification,” in Proc. CVPR, 2022, pp. 4858–4867.\n[18] Y. Xu, Q. Qian, H. Li, R. Jin, and J. Hu, “Weakly supervised representation learning with coarse labels,” in Proc. ICCV, 2021.\n[19] J. Lv, M. Xu, L. Feng, G. Niu, X. Geng, and M. Sugiyama, “Progressive\nidentification of true labels for partial-label learning,” in Proceedings of\nthe 37th International Conference on Machine Learning, ser. Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, vol. 119, 2020.\n[20] Z. Ou, “A review of learning with deep generative models from\nperspective of graphical modeling,” 2018.\n[21] X. Shi, Q. Feng, and L. Xie, “The ASRU 2019 Mandarin-English codeswitching speech recognition challenge: Open datasets, tracks, methods\nand results,” 2020.\n[22] J. Zhou, Y. Guo, S. Zhao, H. Sun, H. Wang, J. He, A. Kong, S. Wang,\nX. Yang, Y. Wang, Y. Lin, and Y. Qin, “CS-Dialogue: A 104-hour dataset\nof spontaneous Mandarin-English code-switching dialogues for speech\nrecognition,” 2025.\n\n\f", "numLines": 788, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 788}}}
